{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3175","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3175","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3175\",\"parent_revision\":\"db0e5037a6536f321686e9002a1c743d390aa06fb1420f92ad018e34db8df0bd\",\"root_revision\":\"db0e5037a6536f321686e9002a1c743d390aa06fb1420f92ad018e34db8df0bd\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P0\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-23\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nA `prek` stash cycle removed 22 markdown edits from the working tree during a commit sequence. The commit reported success, `git status` then read clean, and the edits were present in no commit. No message was emitted at any point.\\n\\nObserved 2026-08-23 during a path-scoped commit sequence over a large unstaged set. The work was recovered in full from `~/.cache/prek/patches/1787447822038-87601.patch` and verified by content before being re-committed, so nothing was lost on this occasion. It was noticed only because the author re-read the files afterwards.\\n\\n`AGENTS.md` gotcha 12 documents this mechanism, and documents a different symptom:\\n\\n> prek stashes unstaged changes before running hooks. If a formatter hook (ruff-format, etc.) modifies staged files and the stash cannot restore cleanly, prek rolls back the hook's changes and the commit fails (\\\"Stashed changes conflicted...\\\").\\n\\nThat describes a loud failure: the commit fails and names the reason. What occurred was the quiet variant \u2014 the commit succeeded and the unstaged edits disappeared. An author following the documented gotcha would have no reason to check, because the documented signal never appears.\\n\\nImpact: work is destroyed silently during a normal, sanctioned operation. The conditions appear to involve a path-scoped commit (`git commit <paths>`) run while a large unstaged set exists, which is precisely the pattern this repository's own commit-cadence guidance prescribes \u2014 commit by explicit file list rather than blanket staging, specifically so a concurrent agent's in-progress edits are not swept in. So the guidance that protects against one hazard steers directly into this one.\\n\\nNothing in the repository records that `~/.cache/prek/patches/` holds the stashed content, so an author who does not know to look there has no recovery path and no indication one exists.\\n\\nNot established: the exact trigger conditions, whether the unstaged set size is causal or incidental, whether the behaviour is version-specific to the installed `prek`, and whether the patch file is retained reliably or is subject to cleanup.\\n\\n**User-provided context**: the reporting agent's proposed mitigation was to commit or stage all intended work before running a path-scoped commit over a large unstaged set. Recorded as a hypothesis for grooming to validate, not as a requirement \u2014 it may trade this hazard for the concurrent-agent one the path-scoped guidance exists to prevent.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: The repository runs prek 0.4.11 through an installed pre-commit hook.\\nevidence: `uv run prek --version` returned `prek 0.4.11`; `.git/hooks/pre-commit` invokes `prek hook-impl`.\\nsource: `uv.lock`, `.git/hooks/pre-commit`, 2026-08-26.\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: The named recovery patch exists and contains substantial captured edits.\\nevidence: `~/.cache/prek/patches/1787447822038-87601.patch` exists and contains 25 file diffs.\\nsource: local prek patch cache, 2026-08-26.\\n\\nverdict: INCONCLUSIVE\\nclaim: Prek 0.4.11 can report a successful commit while silently discarding unstaged edits.\\nevidence: the patch proves captured content, but not the exact command, exit status, timing, or restoration failure.\\nsource: issue incident record and local patch cache, 2026-08-26.\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Final: fix: prek stash cycle silently discards unstaged edits while the commit reports success\\nDate: 2026-08-26\\nGoal: prepare an evidence-backed investigation that preserves working-tree safety.\\nConditions:\\n1. Installed prek version and active hook wiring | Snapshot: AVAILABLE -> Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: `uv run prek --version` returned 0.4.11; `.git/hooks/pre-commit` invokes `prek hook-impl`.\\n2. Recovery evidence for the reported incident | Snapshot: AVAILABLE -> Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: `~/.cache/prek/patches/1787447822038-87601.patch` exists and contains 25 diffs.\\n3. Safety boundary for investigation | Snapshot: DERIVABLE -> Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: `.claude/rules/commit-cadence-and-worktrees.md` requires isolated worktrees; `AGENTS.md` requires hooks to remain enabled.\\nChanges from snapshot:\\n- Exact trigger and silent-success behavior are acceptance criteria for the bounded reproducer, not missing planning input.\\n- Investigation safety moved from DERIVABLE to AVAILABLE from repository policy.\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n</div>\\n\\n## Issue Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nType: defect\\nRationale: A normal repository workflow reportedly produced data loss; the causal chain is traceable but the exact trigger is not yet reproduced.\\nAnalysis Method: 5-whys after a controlled reproducer distinguishes upstream prek behavior from repository-specific hook and policy conditions.\\nScenario Target: mixed staged and unstaged edits during a hook-backed commit -> unrelated edits remain intact and any unsafe transaction fails loudly.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Files\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- `.pre-commit-config.yaml`\\n- `AGENTS.md`\\n- `.claude/rules/commit-cadence-and-worktrees.md`\\n- `pyproject.toml`\\n- `uv.lock`\\n- `scripts/repair_symlinks.py`\\n- `plugins/plugin-creator/scripts/auto_sync_manifests.py`\\n</div>\\n\\n## Resources\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- Issue #3175 incident record.\\n- Prek configuration reference: https://prek.j178.dev/reference/configuration/\\n- Prek CLI reference: https://prek.j178.dev/reference/cli/\\n- Local recovery artifact: `~/.cache/prek/patches/1787447822038-87601.patch`.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nPotential silent loss affects every contributor or agent committing while unrelated edits exist. A loud abort is preferable to a successful commit that leaves an apparently clean tree with recoverable-only content.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Code - Producers\\n- `.pre-commit-config.yaml` - defines the pre-commit transaction and multiple file-mutating hooks.\\n- `scripts/repair_symlinks.py` - writes repairs during that transaction.\\n- `plugins/plugin-creator/scripts/auto_sync_manifests.py` - writes and stages derived manifests from staged changes.\\n\\n### Documentation\\n- `AGENTS.md` - documents the loud stash-conflict path but not the reported silent-success incident or recovery evidence.\\n- `.claude/rules/commit-cadence-and-worktrees.md` - defines the commit/worktree policy that must remain safe.\\n- `CONTRIBUTING.md` and `plugins/plugin-creator/scripts/README-auto-sync.md` - describe commit-time hook behavior.\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n- `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock` - declare and lock prek 0.4.11.\\n- `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml` - consumes the same prek configuration in CI; it validates committed state but cannot recover local edits.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\n- `AGENTS.md` and `.claude/rules/commit-cadence-and-worktrees.md`.\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n- Git hook shim -> prek stash/restore transaction -> mutating hooks -> working tree and index.\\n- GitHub/CI runs the same configured checks after commit, outside the local stash/restore transaction.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nNone identified. The planned reproducer must run in a disposable worktree and must not use a user's active working tree.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Priority\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nPriority: P0\\nEffort: Small discovery-and-guardrail investigation, with scope reassessed after the minimal reproducer identifies the failure owner.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Benefits\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nA verified guard or corrected workflow makes data preservation observable and gives contributors a supported recovery path when the hook transaction cannot restore state.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAlignment assessment: ALIGNED\\nThe current design intentionally keeps hooks enabled, uses explicit file-scoped commits, and isolates concurrent writers in worktrees. Any remedy must preserve those properties rather than bypass hooks or blanket-stage unrelated work.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] A disposable-worktree reproducer records the commit exit status, staged diff, unstaged diff, and patch-cache result for overlapping and non-overlapping staged/unstaged edits.\\n- [ ] The investigation identifies whether the observed failure is reproducible on locked prek 0.4.11 and whether it is upstream behavior, repository interaction, or both.\\n- [ ] The selected remedy preserves unrelated concurrent edits and keeps required hooks enabled.\\n- [ ] A regression check proves that a successful hook-backed commit cannot silently remove the fixture's unstaged edits.\\n- [ ] Contributor guidance names the supported recovery evidence only if its existence and retention semantics are verified.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Expected Behavior\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nFor staged and unrelated unstaged edits, the hook transaction either preserves both states exactly or fails with a visible recovery instruction. A successful commit must never leave unstaged edits absent without an explicit, verifiable outcome.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Effort\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nSmall initially: isolate and classify the transaction. Any upstream report, dependency change, or hook-policy revision follows only if the reproducer establishes a concrete failure mode.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Reproducibility\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nA controlled disposable-worktree scenario must create staged and unstaged edits, including an overlapping-file case, execute the installed pre-commit path, and assert the commit result, working-tree/index state, and any patch-cache artifact. The historical incident remains unreproducible from the record alone.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 22 markdown edits recovered from the prek patch cache, 2026-08-23\\n- **Priority**: P0\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-23\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nObserved chain: the repository uses a prek hook transaction with auto-fixing/writing hooks; prek temporarily stashes unstaged changes; restoration interacts with changed working-tree/index state. The direct failure ownership is not yet established. Repository hook writers and shared-tree policy are contributing conditions, not proven root causes.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"55b8f8b81ce27b633cd89c8ea563d6cc53e769ba1ae22203aa764ae7d1154212\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQyRCeA\"}"}